<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Radon testing in Winnipeg is mostly a waiting game. You put a detector in the lowest lived-in level of the house, leave it through at least 91 days of the heating season, then send it off or read it. Health Canada says 3 to 12 months gives the truest picture of your average annual level, and that average is what the 200 Bq/m3 guideline is measured against.</p><p>It's worth the wait here. Of the Winnipeg homes tested in the 2024 Cross-Canada Survey, 37.4% came in at or above 200 Bq/m3, and Health Canada is clear there are no radon-free areas of Canada. Your neighbour's result doesn't tell you yours, so the honest advice is to test first and talk about <a href="/radon-mitigation/">mitigation</a> only if the number calls for it.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Long-term tests, 91 days minimum</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">At least 91 days of the test fall in the heating season, and 3 to 12 months is better. Anything shorter is a screening, and Health Canada recommends repeating it.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Placed where you actually live</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The detector goes in the lowest lived-in level, in a room someone uses more than 4 hours a day, at least 30 cm above the floor and 30 cm from walls. Not the furnace room, and not beside the sump.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Passive lab kits</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Passive long-term detectors, such as alpha-track kits, sit untouched for months and then go to a lab. The lung associations' kit from HomeRadonTest.ca is $70.00 before tax, with shipping both ways and the analysis included.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Digital monitors for screening</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Electronic monitors show a running number, which is handy for a first look. For a long-term result, Health Canada says use one that passed C-NRPP performance testing.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Your result, read plainly</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">We'll go through what your number means against Health Canada's 200 Bq/m3 guideline, and what it doesn't mean. No colour-coded panic charts.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">A sensible retest schedule</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Retest after a mitigation system goes in, after energy retrofits, and when a new floor becomes the lowest lived-in level. Homes with an active system should retest every five years.</p>
      </div>
</div>
