<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">How a proper radon test works in a Winnipeg home</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>The Health Canada method is simple and slow. Test for at least three months, with a minimum of 91 days during the heating season, and ideally for 3 to 12 months. The Manitoba government suggests winter, roughly October to April, when the house is shut tight.</p><p>Placement matters more than people expect. Put the detector in a normal occupancy area of the lowest lived-in level: a finished basement, a basement bedroom, a rec room the kids use every evening. Keep it in the breathing zone, at least 30 cm above the floor and 30 cm from walls or ceilings, and away from:</p><ul><li>sump holes, crawl spaces and exposed foundation cracks</li><li>heat sources and direct sunlight</li><li>vents, doors and windows</li></ul><p>The usual failures are dull ones. A kit left in the laundry room or an unfinished basement measures a space nobody lives in, and a test pulled before 91 days doesn't answer the annual-average question. Health Canada's advice for a short test is simply to do it again.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Lab kits vs digital monitors</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>You'll run into two classes of equipment. Passive long-term detectors, such as alpha-track kits, stay put for the whole test and then go to a lab; the kits sold through the provincial lung associations are analysed by the Saskatchewan Research Council. HomeRadonTest.ca charges $70.00 per kit before tax, and the Manitoba government puts retail kits at between $50 and $100.</p><p>Digital monitors give a live reading and work well for screening. The Winnipeg Public Library lends them free from all 20 branches between 1 November and 30 April, on a three-week loan with no renewals and a library card in good standing. If you want a monitor for a long-term test, Health Canada says choose one that passed C-NRPP performance testing.</p><p>One practical catch: the Lungs Matter grant needs a long-term 90-day test from a C-NRPP approved kit, and homeowner-level digital monitors don't qualify. If there's any chance you'll apply for help with a fix, use a lab kit for the test that counts.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What 200 Bq/m3 actually means</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Radon is measured in becquerels per cubic metre, written Bq/m3. US kits and apps may show pCi/L instead: 37 Bq/m3 is 1 pCi/L, so Canada's 200 is about 5.4 pCi/L. The 200 figure is Health Canada's guideline for the average annual level in the lived-in area. It isn't a legal limit, and no Manitoba or Saskatchewan law requires a homeowner to test or fix.</p><p>Above 200, Health Canada recommends corrective action within 1 year. Below 200, it also says there is no level considered risk free, so a result just under the line is a lower number, not a clean bill of health.</p><p>Why anyone bothers: Health Canada calls radon the number one cause of lung cancer in non-smokers and estimates it's behind more than 3,000 lung cancer deaths in Canada each year. That's the one grim sentence on this page. The rest is logistics.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Why one short reading shouldn't decide a $2,500 job</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Health Canada is blunt about this. Short-term measurements "are not appropriate for informing mitigation decisions", because the 200 Bq/m3 guideline applies to your average annual level, not to a few days of readings.</p><p>The money is real. Winnipeg installers advertise systems from about $1,500 to $3,000; a Regina installer quotes $2,500 to $3,500. A free 15-minute screening, which one Winnipeg firm offers, or a week on a library monitor is a good reason to start a long-term test. It isn't a reason to sign a contract.</p><p>If your long-term number comes back over 200, read <a href="/radon-mitigation-cost/">what mitigation costs</a> and how <a href="/radon-mitigation/">sub-slab depressurization</a> works. Buying or selling on a deadline? The <a href="/radon-testing-home-sale/">home sale testing page</a> covers what a short screening can and can't tell you. Or call now on (204) 555-0173 and we'll talk it through.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF2F7;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">How long does a radon test take in Winnipeg?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">At least 3 months, with a minimum of 91 days during the heating season. Health Canada says 3 to 12 months gives the most accurate annual average. A test shorter than 91 days should be repeated before you decide anything on it.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does a radon test cost?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">A long-term kit from HomeRadonTest.ca, the ordering site the provincial lung associations use, is $70.00 before tax, including shipping to and from the lab and the analysis. The Manitoba government says retail kits generally cost between $50 and $100. We found no published Prairie price for a professionally placed long-term test, so a radon measurement professional will quote that.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I borrow a radon detector from the Winnipeg library?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Yes. Every Winnipeg Public Library branch lends a digital screening monitor free from 1 November to 30 April, for three weeks with no renewals. CBC reported the monitor should sit at the lowest level of the home for at least seven days. It's a screening tool, so follow a worrying reading with a 91-day test.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">My long-term result is over 200 Bq/m3. What now?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Health Canada recommends corrective action within 1 year, by a C-NRPP certified mitigation professional you can check in C-NRPP's online directory. In Manitoba, Lungs Matter offers up to $1,500 to low- or moderate-income households with a qualifying test and quote, though funding is limited and it doesn't cover work already done. Manitoba Hydro's Home Energy Efficiency Loan covers radon mitigation up to $5,000 over 5 years at 6.2% (O.A.C.).</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Should I test for radon when buying a house?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">We found no Manitoba or Saskatchewan rule requiring radon testing or disclosure at sale. C-NRPP says a short-term screening could give some insight during a sale, followed by a long-term test of at least 91 days after you move in. Conditions, holdbacks and disclosure are questions for your realtor or lawyer.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
